## 5. Telephone normalization (E.164)

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/TelephoneE164.java` — `toE164(raw)`: strips spaces/dashes/brackets, assumes `+61` if no `+` (drops one leading `0`), enforces 8–15 total digits, and enforces exact national length for known codes (`+1`→10, `+61`→9). Returns `null` when invalid. Invoked from `ValidateOwnerFields.normalizeTelephone`, which stores the E.164 form back on the request or adds a `"telephone"` 400 error.

## 6. Email normalization + disposable-domain blocklist

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidateOwnerFields.java`

```java
private static final Pattern EMAIL = Pattern.compile("^[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@]+\\.[^\\s@]+$");

/** Disposable email domains rejected outright: an email in one of these is a 400. */
private static final Set<String> DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS =
        Set.of("mailinator.com", "tempmail.com", "guerrillamail.com");
```
```java
private static void normalizeEmail(OwnerFieldsDto request, List<String> errors) {
    String email = request.getEmail();
    if (email == null || email.isBlank()) return;
    String trimmed = email.trim();
    if (EMAIL.matcher(trimmed).matches() && !isDisposableDomain(trimmed)) {
        request.setEmail(trimmed.toLowerCase());
    } else if (!errors.contains("email")) {
        errors.add("email");
    }
}

private static boolean isDisposableDomain(String email) {
    int at = email.lastIndexOf('@');
    if (at < 0) return false;
    return DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS.contains(email.substring(at + 1).toLowerCase());
}
```
(Email is optional; when present it is validated, blocklist-checked, and stored lower-cased.)

## 7. OfficeFloor YAML for POST owners

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`

The wired create pipeline (in order):
`checkIdempotency` → `validate` (ValidateOwnerFields) → `checkCityCapacity` → `checkDailyLimit` → `build` (BuildOwner) → `assignHousehold` (AssignHousehold) → `assignHouseholdSize` → `assignMembershipLevelCap` → `assignCustomerCode` → `assignNamesakeCount` → `assignBulkSignupWarning` → `assignPossibleDuplicate` (AssignPossibleDuplicate) → `save` → `record` → `audit` → `respond`. The idempotent `existing` branch goes to `respondExisting`.

IMPORTANT: `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` (the 409 household duplicate block) is NOT referenced in this YAML — there is no node that instantiates it, even though its Javadoc and the YAML comment at `assignPossibleDuplicate` ("after checkIdentity has rejected/admitted the household duplicate") both assume it runs. See discrepancy note.

## 8. How owners are flagged "deleted" (soft delete)

Model field `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` (lines 97-98, 247-253):
```java
@Column(name = "deleted", nullable = false)
private boolean deleted;
public boolean isDeleted() { return this.deleted; }
public void setDeleted(boolean deleted) { this.deleted = deleted; }
```

Delete logic `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/DeleteOwner.java`:
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    // Soft delete: retain the record, flag it deleted...
    owner.setDeleted(true);
    ownerRepository.save(owner);
}
```
Wired via `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners/{ownerId}.DELETE.yml` (`load` → `delete` → `respond` 204). Soft-deleted owners are skipped by both `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` and `AssignPossibleDuplicate`.

## 9. Tests related to duplicate detection / identity key

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (the ONLY test file touching this area):

```java
/** identity-key-v2: identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone | lowerEmail |
 * soundex(lastName)); duplicate detection (409) uses it. The exact key depends on soundex, so the
 * deterministic oracle is the collision (identical identity -> 409) plus the exact 64-hex shape. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

    @Test
    void coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex() throws Exception {
        JsonNode r = fetchOwner(createOwnerOk(structuredOwner()));
        String key = r.get("identityKey").asText();
        assertTrue(key.matches("[0-9a-f]{64}"), key);
    }

    @Test
    void coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity() throws Exception {
        ObjectNode a = structuredOwner();
        a.put("email", uniqueEmail());
        createOwnerOk(a);
        ObjectNode b = a.deepCopy(); // identical telephone / email / lastName -> same v2 key
        createOwner(b).andExpect(status().isConflict());
    }
}
```

Test helpers (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`): `structuredOwner()` builds an owner with a unique lastName, unique telephone, `addressLine1`, and fixed `postcode "2000"`; `createOwnerOk` asserts 2xx and returns the id; `createOwner` POSTs to `/api/owners`; `uniqueEmail()` → `owner<seq>@example.test`. No test references `possibleDuplicate`, `sharesHousehold`, `householdId`, or soft-delete behavior directly.

---

## Discrepancy note (important for your change)

There are two internal contradictions between the test/oracle documentation and the actual production code:

1. `AcceptanceTest.coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` asserts `identityKey` matches `[0-9a-f]{64}` (a full SHA-256 hex). But the actual `IdentityKey.of()` returns `telephone|email|householdId` (e.g. `+61...|owner1@example.test|<12 hex>`), which is NOT 64 hex chars. The test doc block describes an "identity-key-v2" using `soundex(lastName)` — there is NO soundex implementation anywhere in `src/main` (grep for "soundex" only hits this test file).

2. `owners.POST.yml` does not wire `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` (`checkIdentity`) into the pipeline, yet `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` expects a 409 on a duplicate, and the YAML/Javadoc comments reference a `checkIdentity` step that no longer exists in the composition. So as currently wired, the 409 duplicate path may not execute.

These strongly suggest an in-progress migration toward a soundex-based 64-hex SHA identity key with a re-wired duplicate check.

